# nadia.mcp — tools nadia does not ship

The bar for a seventh built-in tool stays where it is (`SPEC.md` §2). MCP is the other
answer to "I need one more": tools nadia does not define and is not responsible for,
connected by the operator for a run.

The tier below already has the hard part. `std.mcp.client` speaks the protocol and
`std.agent.mcp.mcpToolSource` already wraps a connected server's tools as `AgentTool`s —
so what lives here is only what an application owns: where the config is, which servers
are connected, what the tools are called, and what happens when a server is broken.

One limit of this backend is stated rather than hidden: `Transport.Spawn` carries a
command and arguments, but no environment. A config entry with a non-empty `env` is
**refused by name** rather than connected without it — a server started without the token
its config sets fails later, further away, and looks like the server's fault. The Rust
implementation passes `env`; this one says why it cannot.

`std.mcp.client` documents `Feature.McpClient` as jvm/js-only, which reads as "not under
`ssc run`". Measured 2026-08-01: it connects and calls fine on the v2 VM — a real server
(`rozum mcp-proxy`) listed its seven tools and the model called one end to end. The
connect failure below therefore reports what actually went wrong instead of blaming the
backend, which is what that stale note would have had it do.

[AgentTool, agentTool, toolOk, toolError](std/agent.ssc)
[mcpToolSource](std/agent-mcp.ssc)
[McpClient, mcpConnect, Transport, McpError](std/mcp/client.ssc)
[readFile, exists](std/fs.ssc)

```scalascript
// An object parses to a Map; the config's server names are its keys. `std.json`'s
// navigable JsonValue cannot enumerate keys, and the names are exactly what we need.
extern def jsonParse(s: String): Map[String, Any]

/** One `mcpServers` entry. `url`/`kind` are only ever used to explain a refusal. */
case class McpServerSpec(
  name: String,
  command: String,
  args: List[String],
  envKeys: List[String],
  url: String,
  kind: String
)

def asText(v: Any): String =
  v match
    case s: String => s
    case _         => ""

def asTextList(v: Any): List[String] =
  v match
    case l: List[Any] => l.map(asText).filter(s => s != "")
    case _            => List()

def asKeys(v: Any): List[String] =
  v match
    case m: Map[String, Any] => m.keys.toList.sorted
    case _                   => List()

/** Read the ecosystem's `mcpServers` object — the shape Claude Code and the rest already
  * write, so an operator's existing file works unchanged and nobody learns a nadia config. */
def parseMcpConfig(text: String): List[McpServerSpec] =
  val root = jsonParse(text)
  root.get("mcpServers") match
    case Some(servers) =>
      servers match
        case m: Map[String, Any] =>
          m.keys.toList.sorted.map { name =>
            val entry = m.get(name).map(e => e).getOrElse("")
            entry match
              case e: Map[String, Any] =>
                McpServerSpec(
                  name,
                  asText(e.get("command").getOrElse("")),
                  asTextList(e.get("args").getOrElse(List())),
                  asKeys(e.get("env").getOrElse("")),
                  asText(e.get("url").getOrElse("")),
                  asText(e.get("type").getOrElse(""))
                )
              case _ => McpServerSpec(name, "", List(), List(), "", "")
          }
        case _ => List()
    case None => List()

/** Why this entry cannot be a stdio server, or `None` when it can. Refusing by name is
  * the point: an operator who configured an HTTP server and saw no error would conclude
  * it was connected. */
def stdioRefusal(spec: McpServerSpec): Option[String] =
  if spec.command.trim() == "" then
    val what =
      if spec.url != "" then "a url (" + spec.url + ")"
      else if spec.kind != "" then "type `" + spec.kind + "`"
      else "no `command`"
    Some("MCP server `" + spec.name + "` has " + what + ": nadia speaks the stdio transport " +
         "only, so this server cannot be connected. Give it a `command` that starts the " +
         "server on stdio, or drop it from --mcp.")
  else if spec.envKeys.length > 0 then
    Some("MCP server `" + spec.name + "` sets env (" + spec.envKeys.mkString(", ") + "), and " +
         "this backend's Spawn transport cannot pass it to the child. Refusing rather than " +
         "starting the server without it — a server missing its token fails later and looks " +
         "like the server's fault. Export the variables before starting nadia, or use the " +
         "Rust implementation, which passes them.")
  else None

/** Which servers to connect, from `--mcp NAME…` / `--mcp-all`. A name that is not in the
  * config is an error naming what IS there: a typo must not read as "no tools". */
def selectServers(all: List[McpServerSpec], wanted: List[String], everyOne: Boolean): Either[String, List[McpServerSpec]] =
  if everyOne then Right(all)
  else
    def go(rest: List[String], acc: List[McpServerSpec]): Either[String, List[McpServerSpec]] =
      rest match
        case Nil => Right(acc)
        case name :: tail =>
          all.find(s => s.name == name) match
            case Some(s) => go(tail, acc :+ s)
            case None =>
              val known = all.map(s => s.name)
              Left(
                if known.length == 0 then "no MCP server `" + name + "` — the config has none configured"
                else "no MCP server `" + name + "` — the config has: " + known.mkString(" ")
              )
    go(wanted, List())

/** The name an MCP tool carries into the model's tool list: the ecosystem's convention.
  * The prefix makes a collision with the six impossible and keeps two servers apart. */
def mcpPrefixed(server: String, tool: String): String = "mcp__" + server + "__" + tool

def isMcpTool(name: String): Boolean = name.startsWith("mcp__")

/** Connect one server and return its tools, renamed. Renaming the `AgentTool` is enough:
  * its handler closed over the server's own name when `mcpToolSource` built it, so the
  * call still goes out under the real name while the model sees the prefixed one. */
def connectServer(spec: McpServerSpec): Either[String, List[AgentTool]] =
  stdioRefusal(spec) match
    case Some(why) => Left(why)
    case None =>
      try
        val client = mcpConnect(Transport.Spawn(spec.command, spec.args))
        Right(mcpToolSource(client).map { t =>
          AgentTool(mcpPrefixed(spec.name, t.name), t.description, t.parametersJson, t.handler)
        })
      catch
        case e: McpError =>
          Left("MCP server `" + spec.name + "`: " + e.toString)
        case e: Throwable =>
          // Anything else the spawn or the handshake can raise — a command that is not on
          // PATH is the common one. It reaches the operator verbatim, with the command, so
          // the answer is in the message rather than in a debugging session.
          Left("MCP server `" + spec.name + "` (" + spec.command + "): " + e.toString)

/** The line printed once per connected server: what it added, and that it is NOT jailed. */
def connectedLine(name: String, tools: List[AgentTool]): String =
  "mcp `" + name + "`: " + tools.length.toString + " tool(s) — " +
  tools.map(t => t.name).mkString(" ") + " · runs OUTSIDE the workspace jail"
```
